Implement the feature:
“Publicly Available Form (for Oakes Award?)”

BUSINESS REQUIREMENT:
Users who have not yet completed the TOW kickoff/onboarding should still be able to submit an Oakes Award recommendation.

The ticket also mentions a possible broader solution:
Allow users to sign up/access the application as a Viewer so they can access limited public functionality.

IMPORTANT:
Do not start coding immediately.

First analyze the existing application and determine:
1. Current authentication/login flow.
2. Viewer role and its existing permissions.
3. User onboarding/TOW kickoff restrictions.
4. Existing recommendation/nomination or form-submission functionality.
5. Whether an Oakes Award form or related Goal/List already exists.
6. How public/unauthenticated routes are currently secured.
7. Existing APIs, database tables, RLS/security policies, notifications, and audit logging that can be reused.

Then implement the safest solution that satisfies the requirement.

PREFERRED FUNCTIONAL BEHAVIOR:

A. Oakes Award Recommendation Form
- Provide an easy way for an eligible user who has not completed TOW onboarding to submit an Oakes Award recommendation.
- Do not require full TOW application access just to submit the recommendation.
- Reuse the existing application design and form components where possible.
- Clearly validate required fields.
- Show a clear success confirmation after submission.
- Prevent duplicate submissions caused by double-click/retries.
- Do not expose internal application data to users who only have form access.

B. Authentication / Viewer Access
Before creating any new auth model, inspect whether the existing Viewer role can safely support this workflow.

If Viewer access is the intended existing architecture:
- Allow the minimum necessary signup/access flow.
- Viewer must have read-only/limited permissions.
- Viewer must NOT automatically gain access to private teams, goals, items, admin pages, or sensitive records.
- Viewer should only access explicitly public functionality and the Oakes Award submission flow.

Do NOT automatically make every external/unauthenticated user a full application user.

C. Submission Destination
- Determine from the existing code/data where Oakes Award recommendations should be stored.
- If there is an existing Team/List/Goal/workflow for recommendations, reuse it.
- Do not create a parallel duplicate workflow unnecessarily.
- Preserve ownership, team/list association, status, audit information, and notifications according to existing patterns.

D. Security
This is critical:
- Enforce permissions server-side, not only in UI.
- Validate and sanitize all public form inputs.
- Add rate limiting / abuse protection using existing project patterns if available.
- Do not expose service-role credentials or privileged APIs.
- Respect Supabase RLS/security architecture.
- Avoid enumeration of users, teams, or internal data.
- Log/audit submissions appropriately.

E. UX
The user journey should be simple:

User opens Oakes Award recommendation link
        ↓
Allowed to access recommendation form
        ↓
Completes required information
        ↓
Submits
        ↓
Clear confirmation
        ↓
Authorized internal users can review it in the normal TOW workflow

Do not force an un-onboarded user through unrelated TOW functionality.

